{"parentUuid":"2ce35421-8bf1-4536-8b85-6cd5cd1a7554","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d7fd903f-4eab-4718-8a3d-1c392317615c","agentId":"a1c65c960a2f9cb0b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012i8PzuSewKzfenkFsUUbYB","type":"tool_result","content":"Based on the provided content, here's what the CP-SAT Primer addresses:\n\n**(1) Detecting search stalling:** The document doesn't explicitly discuss stalling detection metrics or thresholds.\n\n**(2) Typical metrics (conflicts/sec, propagations/sec):** The primer doesn't provide performance benchmarks or per-second rate calculations for these metrics.\n\n**(3) Signs of no progress in long-running solves:** The document notes that the search log shows \"where it faces significant challenges\" and distinguishes whether \"the upper or lower bounds are causing issues,\" but doesn't specify indicators that a solver has stalled.\n\n**(4) Best subsolvers for proving infeasibility:** The primer doesn't recommend specific subsolvers for infeasibility detection.\n\n**(5) Case studies with hour-scale runtimes:** No case studies are included in this chapter.\n\n**What IS covered:** The primer explains how to interpret the summary section, which reports the final status: \"FEASIBLE, OPTIMAL, INFEASIBLE, UNKNOWN, or MODEL_INVALID.\" It also mentions the \"gap_integral\" metric as helpful—\"A lower value will indicate that the solver is converging faster\"—but provides no guidance on interpreting long-duration runs.\n\nThe document focuses on *reading and understanding logs* rather than troubleshooting performance issues in extended solves."}]},"uuid":"67431324-143e-4603-9f63-ff738c210681","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:31:04.100Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"2ce35421-8bf1-4536-8b85-6cd5cd1a7554","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"b5d516eb-16c9-4fda-8ecc-a7dace4d3979","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d7fd903f-4eab-4718-8a3d-1c392317615c","agentId":"a1c65c960a2f9cb0b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01STt4uZwUfGPY81WLTjvhsW","type":"tool_result","content":"I've reviewed the GitHub page content you provided, which shows the repository structure and metadata for the CP-SAT Primer project. However, **the actual content of the chapters, examples, and documentation is not included in this page snapshot** — only the navigation menus and repository layout are visible.\n\nThe page displays:\n- Repository name: \"The CP-SAT Primer: Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\"\n- Main topics: optimization, operations-research, ortools, combinatorial-optimization, cp-sat\n- File structure includes `chapters/`, `examples/`, and `evaluations/` directories\n- 729 stars, 59 forks\n\n**I cannot locate** the specific sections you're asking about (long-running CP-SAT, proving infeasibility, war stories, decomposition/Benders, hint usage, num_workers tuning, LNS, hard problems, or any industrial case studies with specific numbers) because the actual chapter content isn't present in this page screenshot.\n\nTo find those topics, you would need to either:\n1. Visit the live documentation at `d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/`\n2. Browse the `chapters/` folder directly in the repository\n3. Check the example files in the `examples/` directory\n\nWould you like to provide the actual chapter content so I can search for those specific sections?"}]},"uuid":"c0b626bb-4807-4632-9d6c-4a3f8dc51c52","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:31:06.408Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b5d516eb-16c9-4fda-8ecc-a7dace4d3979","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"dcad242e-a5b4-46fe-a6bf-2bb60d9b6e79","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d7fd903f-4eab-4718-8a3d-1c392317615c","agentId":"a1c65c960a2f9cb0b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EFsiwPjnjDsn6mm7upQwKK","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"CP-SAT\" \"core\" \"max_lp\" \"lb_tree_search\" infeasibility subsolver portfolio\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Parameters - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/05_parameters.html\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Custom combination of subsolvers\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/pQ4B7QD6ebw\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT: Infeasibility Subsolvers\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/Tz8ovW6cZtY\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Solver | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/cp/cp_solver\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT claims to find optimal solution to a MILP\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/4fcAuWiIUFA\"},{\"title\":\"How does it work? - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/07_under_the_hood.html\"},{\"title\":\"Understanding the Log - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/understanding_the_log.html\"},{\"title\":\"trying to solve huge mode on CP-Sat\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/VjiSYjFFB_4\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT solver couldn't prove the solution is optimal if num_workers < 5 but otherwise it did instantly. · Issue #3662 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/3662\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - d-krupke/cpsat-primer: The CP-SAT Primer: Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/d-krupke/cpsat-primer\"}]\n\nI found relevant information about CP-SAT subsolvers related to your search query. Here's what I found:\n\n## Key CP-SAT Subsolvers\n\nThe \"core\" subsolver is classified as a complete solver, and \"max_lp\" is the same as default_lp but with maximal linearization.\n\n\"lb_tree_search\" is a solver focused on improving the proven bound, not on finding better solutions. By disproving the feasibility of the cheapest nodes in the search tree, it incrementally improves the bound, but has only little chances to find better solutions.\n\n## Portfolio Approach\n\nCP-SAT uses a portfolio of different subsolvers, each configured with varying settings (e.g., different levels of linearization) to solve the model. CP-SAT is a versatile portfolio solver, centered around a Lazy Clause Generation (LCG) based Constraint Programming Solver. In its role as a portfolio solver, CP-SAT concurrently executes a multitude of diverse algorithms and strategies, each possessing unique strengths and weaknesses. These elements operate largely independently but engage in information exchange, sharing progress when better solutions emerge or tighter bounds become available.\n\n## Infeasibility Subsolvers\n\nRegarding infeasibility solving, recommended subsolvers include: Core, max_lp, probing*, lb_tree_search, *costs, and maybe no_lp.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"3e2236cc-97c5-4235-bd72-ec11fc35f6af","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:31:09.570Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"dcad242e-a5b4-46fe-a6bf-2bb60d9b6e79","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
