{"parentUuid":"d165ce7d-eac8-4a4f-8d2c-4897300c997c","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d7fd903f-4eab-4718-8a3d-1c392317615c","agentId":"a1c65c960a2f9cb0b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UMbNej2BPvawpZptc39zw9","type":"tool_result","content":"# Summary of CP-SAT Discussion\n\n## Problem Being Solved\nJosh Friedman is attempting to solve a constraint programming model with **500,000 to 1,000,000 boolean variables** using Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT solver.\n\n## Problem Size Details\nThe actual model that ran had:\n- 89,786 variables (59,340 in objective)\n- 735,777 boolean OR constraints\n- 7,580 linear constraints\n\n## The Issue\nThe solver appeared to complete immediately without producing results. When Friedman reduced the model size, it worked properly.\n\n## Recommendations Given\n\n**Laurent Perron's primary advice:**\n> \"add solver.parameters.log_search_progress = True most likely, your problem is infeasible.\"\n\n**For diagnosing infeasibility, Perron recommended:**\n1. Export the model using `model.ExportToFile(...)`\n2. Run the `sat_runner` binary with verbose logging to see presolve steps where infeasibility occurs\n\n**For large models (6GB+):**\n- Joshua Friedman suggested increasing swap space for memory-intensive operations\n- Perron noted there is a 2GB limit on protocol buffer internal size, which can cause segmentation faults with very large exported models\n\n## Resolution\nFriedman ultimately discovered a mistake in his code causing the infeasibility, which he corrected."}]},"uuid":"81a3c6a9-9635-4b6d-8f83-6a661229393c","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:31:20.185Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d165ce7d-eac8-4a4f-8d2c-4897300c997c","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"97aa47fd-aa13-4228-ba79-8d25102d3d91","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d7fd903f-4eab-4718-8a3d-1c392317615c","agentId":"a1c65c960a2f9cb0b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011iB97CcTDUXNRsEimJa9TR","type":"tool_result","content":"# Issue Summary\n\nThe problem reported is that the CP-SAT solver failed to **prove optimality** when `num_workers < 5`, even though it found the correct optimal solution value. With 4 workers, the solver ran for the full 60 seconds without proving the solution was optimal, while with 5+ workers it proved optimality instantly.\n\n## Laurent Perron's Response\n\nI cannot locate Laurent Perron's reply in the provided webpage content. The page shows the issue was assigned to him and milestoned for v9.6, but his actual response or explanation about the relationship between `num_workers` and optimality/infeasibility proofs is not included in the extracted content.\n\nTo provide the exact quote you requested, I would need access to the comments section of this GitHub issue, which does not appear in the material provided."}]},"uuid":"5534cbea-8833-4d3d-a89d-c77204a17e8a","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:31:21.059Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"97aa47fd-aa13-4228-ba79-8d25102d3d91","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"a68a60b6-4cc7-4669-bd49-185d598a0fe2","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d7fd903f-4eab-4718-8a3d-1c392317615c","agentId":"a1c65c960a2f9cb0b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Pd1zQ6LbSdrr9Jhbjv926q","type":"tool_result","content":"# CP-SAT Technical Guidance from the Primer\n\n## Portfolio Solver Strategy\nCP-SAT operates as a portfolio solver running \"diverse algorithms and strategies\" concurrently across threads, rather than optimizing a single approach. The document notes this redundancy proves effective because \"predicting which algorithm is best suited to solve a given problem\" remains challenging.\n\n## Proving Infeasibility/Optimality\nThe solver achieves provable results through \"linear relaxations and cutting planes\" plus SAT-based constraint propagation. At the root level, \"cutting planes such as Gomory-Cuts are applied to improve the linear relaxation,\" which helps detect infeasibility and establish bounds.\n\n## Long-Running Solves & LNS\nFor extended solving, CP-SAT employs Large Neighborhood Search (LNS) heuristics. The process involves: making \"a copy of the model,\" fixing variables, then \"the simplified model is presolved with the fixed variables, which often makes the model much easier to solve.\" LNS iterates \"via a Round Robin approach\" across different neighborhood strategies.\n\n## When CP-SAT Struggles\nThe primer explicitly states CP-SAT has limitations:\n\n- **MIP problems**: \"may not outpace dedicated MIP-solvers in terms of speed\"\n- **Continuous variables**: \"lacks support for continuous variables\" and workarounds \"may not always be highly efficient\"\n- **Specific constraints**: \"may exhibit inefficiency when confronted with certain constraints, such as modulo constraints\"\n- **Interior point methods**: Not available, limiting polynomial-time algorithms for quadratic constraints\n\n**No specific worker counts or runtime recommendations appear in this content.**"}]},"uuid":"4194ccda-8638-4921-a6df-1b6a787fc7cf","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:31:23.507Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"a68a60b6-4cc7-4669-bd49-185d598a0fe2","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"38e98c1b-ed9f-4257-baf0-6a17d4e8953e","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d7fd903f-4eab-4718-8a3d-1c392317615c","agentId":"a1c65c960a2f9cb0b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TAhn2tFET7qRs1gNHHKmYS","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"CP-SAT\" hard instance \"hours\" timeout \"no_lp\" \"max_lp\" portfolio optimal\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Parameters - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/05_parameters.html\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Solver | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/cp/cp_solver\"},{\"title\":\"Setting solver limits | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/cp/cp_tasks\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT solver couldn't prove the solution is optimal if num_workers < 5 but otherwise it did instantly. · Issue #3662 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/3662\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Custom combination of subsolvers\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/pQ4B7QD6ebw\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - d-krupke/cpsat-primer: The CP-SAT Primer: Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/d-krupke/cpsat-primer\"},{\"title\":\"Understanding the Log - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/understanding_the_log.html\"},{\"title\":\"trying to solve huge mode on CP-Sat\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/VjiSYjFFB_4\"},{\"title\":\"or-tools/ortools/sat/sat_parameters.proto at stable · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/blob/stable/ortools/sat/sat_parameters.proto\"},{\"title\":\"The CP-SAT-LP Solver Laurent Perron # Google, Paris, France Frédéric Didier #\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol280-cp2023/LIPIcs.CP.2023.3/LIPIcs.CP.2023.3.pdf\"}]\n\nI found relevant search results about CP-SAT and its configuration parameters. Here's what the search revealed:\n\n## CP-SAT Portfolio-Based Approach\n\nCP-SAT is a portfolio-solver that uses different techniques to solve the problem. CP-SAT uses a portfolio of different subsolvers, each configured with varying settings (e.g., different levels of linearization) to solve the model.\n\n## Key Subsolvers and Parameters\n\nThe search results show that CP-SAT includes several important subsolvers:\n\n- `no_lp` (linearization_level:0)\n- `max_lp` (linearization_level:2)\n- `default_lp` (linearization_level:1)\n\n## Performance Considerations for Hard Instances\n\nmax_lp is probably a waste of resources if you know that your model has a terrible linear relaxation. Additionally, Solutions and Objective bounds blocks in the log can show which strategies (like 'no_lp', 'quick_restart', 'quick_restart_no_lp') are contributing solutions and bounds.\n\n## Timeout Control\n\nFor setting time limits on the solver: solver.parameters.max_time_in_seconds can be used to set a timeout limit for achieving an OPTIMAL solution status before terminating.\n\nThe portfolio approach is particularly valuable for hard instances because running different techniques in parallel will increase the chance of running the right technique, as predicting which technique will be the best for a specific problem is often hard.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"604ed0a0-bb3d-4b8b-8678-eaa5a230039d","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:31:26.645Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"38e98c1b-ed9f-4257-baf0-6a17d4e8953e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
